\begin_layout Title
Macroeconomic Theory II: Exam 2 (Spring 2013)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Consider the so-called yeoman farmer economy.
 The economy consists of the monetary authority and a continuum of yeoman
 farmers indexed by 
\begin_inset Formula $j\in[0,1]$
\end_inset

.
 Each yeoman farmer produces a differentiated good with a production technology
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
Y_{t}(j)=A_{t}N_{t}(j)
\]

\end_inset

A yeoman farmer earns income by selling his or her good in the market, recognizi
ng he or she is a monopolisitic supplier of the good.
 The earned income is used either to purcahse goods or to purchase bonds.
 Mathematically, the problem of a yeoman farmer is indexed by 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

 is written as choosing a sequence of consumption for differentiated goods
 
\begin_inset Formula $\{C_{t}(i,j)\}_{i\in[0,1]}$
\end_inset

, the price level of the product he or she is producing 
\begin_inset Formula $P_{t}(j)$
\end_inset

, and the bond holding 
\begin_inset Formula $B_{t}(j)$
\end_inset

 to maximize the lifetime utility
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
E_{0}\left\{ {\displaystyle \sum_{t=0}^{\infty}}\beta^{t}\left(\log\left[C_{t}(j)\right]-\frac{\left(N_{t}(j)\right)^{1+\varphi}}{1+\varphi}\right)\right\} 
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $C_{t}(j)$
\end_inset

 is the consumption index given by
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
C_{t}(j)=\left[{\displaystyle \int_{0}^{1}}C_{t}(i,j)^{\frac{\epsilon-1}{\epsilon}}di\right]^{\frac{\epsilon}{\epsilon-1}}
\]

\end_inset

The yeoman farmer is facing the flow budget constraint,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
{\displaystyle \int_{0}^{1}}P_{t}(i)C_{t}(i,j)di+Q_{t}B_{t}(j)=B_{t-1}(j)+P_{t}(j)Y_{t}(j)
\]

\end_inset

the demand constraint,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
Y_{t}(j)=\left(\frac{P_{t}(j)}{P_{t}}\right)^{-\epsilon}C_{t}
\]

\end_inset

and no-Ponzi game condition.
 We assume that everyone starts with zero initial bond holding, i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $B_{-1}(j)=0$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $j\in[0,1]$
\end_inset

.
 
\begin_inset Formula $P_{t}$
\end_inset

 is aggregate price index defined as
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
P_{t}=\left[{\displaystyle \int_{0}^{1}}P_{t}(i)^{1-\epsilon}di\right]^{\frac{1}{1-\epsilon}}
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $C_{t}$
\end_inset

 is the aggregate demand defined as
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
C_{t}={\displaystyle \int_{0}^{1}}C_{t}(i)di
\]

\end_inset

Both 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\{ P_{t}(i)\right\} _{i\in[0,1],i\neq j}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $C_{t}$
\end_inset

 are taken as given by a yeoman farmer 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

.
 The price of bond 
\begin_inset Formula $Q_{t}$
\end_inset

 is chosen by the monetary authority.
 Answer the following questions.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Problem
First, we will solve the problem of maximization of 
\begin_inset Formula $C_{t}(j)$
\end_inset

 for any given expenditure, i.e.,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\max & C_{t}(j)=\left[{\displaystyle \int_{0}^{1}}C_{t}(i,j)^{\frac{\epsilon-1}{\epsilon}}di\right]^{\frac{\epsilon}{\epsilon-1}}\\
\mbox{s.t.} & {\displaystyle \int_{0}^{1}}P_{t}(i)C_{t}(i,j)di=Z_{t}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Derive the first order condition
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
C_{t}(i,j)=\left(\frac{P_{t}(i)}{P_{t}(i^{\prime})}\right)^{-\epsilon}C_{t}(i^{\prime},j)\ \mbox{for all}\ i,i^{\prime}\in[0,1]
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
First, we set up the Lagrangian
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
L=\left[{\displaystyle \int_{0}^{1}}C_{t}(i,j)^{\frac{\epsilon-1}{\epsilon}}di\right]^{\frac{\epsilon}{\epsilon-1}}+\lambda\left[Z_{t}-{\displaystyle \int_{0}^{1}}P_{t}(i)C_{t}(i,j)di\right]
\]

\end_inset

Taking the derivative of the function with respect to consumption of good
 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 by farmer 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

 at time 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 yields
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\frac{\partial L}{\partial C_{t}(i,j)}=0:\left[{\displaystyle \int_{0}^{1}}C_{t}(i,j)^{\frac{\epsilon-1}{\epsilon}}di\right]^{\frac{1}{\epsilon-1}}C_{t}(i,j)^{-\frac{1}{\epsilon}}-\lambda P_{t}(i)=0
\]

\end_inset

Note that this condition holds for all 
\begin_inset Formula $i\in[0,1]$
\end_inset

, so we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\left(\frac{C_{t}(i,j)}{C_{t}(i^{\prime},j)}\right)^{-\frac{1}{\epsilon}} & = & \frac{P_{t}(i)}{P_{t}(i^{\prime})}\\
C_{t}(i,j) & = & \left(\frac{P_{t}(i)}{P_{t}(i^{\prime})}\right)^{-\epsilon}C_{t}(i^{\prime},j)
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Problem
By substituting the condition you just derived into the expresison for consumpti
on expenditure, derive
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
C_{t}(i,j)=\left(\frac{P_{t}(i)}{P_{t}}\right)^{-\epsilon}\frac{Z_{t}}{P_{t}}\ \mbox{for all}\ i\in[0,1]
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
The expression for consumption expenditure is
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
{\displaystyle \int_{0}^{1}}P_{t}(i)C_{t}(i,j)di=Z_{t}
\]

\end_inset

Substituting the condition derived in problem 1 into this expression yields
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
{\displaystyle \int_{0}^{1}}P_{t}(i)\left(\frac{P_{t}(i)}{P_{t}(i^{\prime})}\right)^{-\epsilon}C_{t}(i^{\prime},j)di & = & Z_{t}\\
C_{t}(i^{\prime},j)P_{t}(i^{\prime})^{\epsilon}{\displaystyle \int_{0}^{1}}P_{t}(i)^{1-\epsilon} & = & Z_{t}\\
C_{t}(i^{\prime},j)P_{t}(i^{\prime})^{\epsilon}P_{t}^{1-\epsilon} & = & Z_{t}\\
C_{t}(i^{\prime},j) & = & \left(\frac{P_{t}(i^{\prime})}{P_{t}}\right)^{-\epsilon}\frac{Z_{t}}{P_{t}}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Problem
By substituting the condition you just derived into the definition of 
\begin_inset Formula $C_{t}(j)$
\end_inset

, show that
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
C_{t}(j)=\frac{Z_{t}}{P_{t}}
\]

\end_inset

and therefore,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
{\displaystyle \int_{0}^{1}}P_{t}(i)C_{t}(i,j)di=P_{t}C_{t}(j)
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
The definition of 
\begin_inset Formula $C_{t}(j)$
\end_inset

 is
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
C_{t}(j)=\left[{\displaystyle \int_{0}^{1}}C_{t}(i,j)^{\frac{\epsilon-1}{\epsilon}}di\right]^{\frac{\epsilon}{\epsilon-1}}
\]

\end_inset

Substituting the condition derived in problem 2 into this expression yields
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
C_{t}(j) & = & \left[{\displaystyle \int_{0}^{1}}\left\{ \left(\frac{P_{t}(i)}{P_{t}}\right)^{-\epsilon}\frac{Z_{t}}{P_{t}}\right\} ^{\frac{\epsilon-1}{\epsilon}}di\right]^{\frac{\epsilon}{\epsilon-1}}\\
 & = & \frac{Z_{t}}{P_{t}}\left[P_{t}^{\epsilon-1}{\displaystyle \int_{0}^{1}}P_{t}(i)^{1-\epsilon}di\right]^{\frac{\epsilon}{\epsilon-1}}\\
 & = & \frac{Z_{t}}{P_{t}}P_{t}^{\epsilon}\left[{\displaystyle \int_{0}^{1}}P_{t}(i)^{1-\epsilon}di\right]^{\frac{\epsilon}{\epsilon-1}}\\
 & = & Z_{t}P_{t}^{\epsilon-1}\left[P_{t}^{1-\epsilon}\right]^{\frac{\epsilon}{\epsilon-1}}\\
C_{t}(j) & = & \frac{Z_{t}}{P_{t}}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Thus,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
{\displaystyle \int_{0}^{1}}P_{t}(i)C_{t}(i,j)di & = & Z_{t}\\
{\displaystyle \int_{0}^{1}}P_{t}(i)C_{t}(i,j)di & = & P_{t}C_{t}(j)
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Now, we can rewrite the problem as choosing a sequence of 
\begin_inset Formula $C_{t}(j)$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $P_{t}(j)$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $B_{t}(j)$
\end_inset

 to maximize
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
E_{0}\left[{\displaystyle \sum_{t=0}^{\infty}}\beta^{t}\left(\log\left[C_{t}(j)\right]-\frac{\left(N_{t}(j)\right)^{1+\varphi}}{1+\varphi}\right)\right]
\]

\end_inset

subject to
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
P_{t}C_{t}(j)+Q_{t}B_{t}(j) & = & B_{t-1}(j)+P_{t}(j)Y_{t}(j)\\
Y_{t}(j) & = & A_{t}N_{t}(j)\\
Y_{t}(j) & = & \left(\frac{P_{t}(j)}{P_{t}}\right)^{-\epsilon}C_{t}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Answer the following questions.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Problem
Derive the first order conditions
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
-\frac{Q_{t}}{P_{t}}\frac{1}{C_{t}(j)}+E_{t}\left[\beta\frac{1}{P_{t+1}}\frac{1}{C_{t+1}(j)}\right] & = & 0\\
\left(-\epsilon\frac{Y_{t}(j)}{P_{t}(j)}\right)\frac{1}{A_{t}}\left[-(N_{t}(j))^{\varphi}\right]+\left[Y_{t}(j)+P_{t}(j)\left(-\epsilon\frac{Y_{t}(j)}{P_{t}(j)}\right)\right]\frac{1}{P_{t}}\frac{1}{C_{t}(j)} & = & 0
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
First, we set up the Lagrangian
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
L & = & E_{0}\left\{ {\displaystyle \sum_{t=0}^{\infty}}\beta^{t}\left[\log\left[C_{t}(j)\right]-\frac{\left(\frac{1}{A_{t}}P_{t}(j)^{-\epsilon}P_{t}^{\epsilon}C_{t}\right)^{1+\varphi}}{1+\varphi}\right.\right.\\
 &  & \left.\left.+\lambda_{t}\left(B_{t-1}(j)+P_{t}(j)^{1-\epsilon}P_{t}^{\epsilon}C_{t}-P_{t}C_{t}(j)-Q_{t}B_{t}(j)\right)\right]\right\} 
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

The first-order conditions are
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{\partial L}{\partial C_{t}(j)}=0:$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\frac{1}{C_{t}(j)P_{t}}=\lambda_{t}
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $\frac{\partial L}{\partial P_{t}(j)}=0:$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
\epsilon\frac{1}{A_{t}}P_{t}(j)^{-\epsilon-1}P_{t}^{\epsilon}C_{t}\left(\frac{1}{A_{t}}P_{t}(j)^{-\epsilon}P_{t}^{\epsilon}C_{t}\right)^{\varphi}+(1-\epsilon)\lambda_{t}P_{t}(j)^{-\epsilon}P_{t}^{\epsilon}C_{t} & = & 0\nonumber \\
\epsilon\frac{Y_{t}(j)}{P_{t}(j)}\frac{1}{A_{t}}\left(N_{t}(j)\right)^{\varphi}+(1-\epsilon)\frac{1}{C_{t}(j)P_{t}}Y_{t}(j) & = & 0\nonumber \\
\left(-\epsilon\frac{Y_{t}(j)}{P_{t}(j)}\right)\frac{1}{A_{t}}\left(-N_{t}(j)\right)^{\varphi}+\left(Y_{t}(j)+P_{t}(j)\left(-\epsilon\frac{Y_{t}(j)}{P_{t}(j)}\right)\right)\frac{1}{P_{t}C_{t}(j)} & = & 0
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $\frac{\partial L}{\partial B_{t}(j)}=0:$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
-\lambda_{t}Q_{t}+\beta E_{t}\left[\lambda_{t+1}\right] & = & 0\\
-\frac{Q_{t}}{P_{t}}\frac{1}{C_{t}(j)}+E_{t}\left[\beta\frac{1}{P_{t+1}}\frac{1}{C_{t+1}(j)}\right] & = & 0
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Problem
Give intuitive explanations for why the above two equations are necessary
 conditions for optimality.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
Will prove.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Problem
Rewriting equation (1), show that the following equation is the first-order
 necessary condition for optimality:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\frac{P_{t}(j)}{P_{t}}=\frac{\epsilon}{\epsilon-1}\frac{1}{A_{t}}C_{t}(j)(N_{t}(j))^{\varphi}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
Rewriting equation (1)
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\left(-\epsilon\frac{Y_{t}(j)}{P_{t}(j)}\right)\frac{1}{A_{t}}\left(-N_{t}(j)\right)^{\varphi}+\left(Y_{t}(j)+P_{t}(j)\left(-\epsilon\frac{Y_{t}(j)}{P_{t}(j)}\right)\right)\frac{1}{P_{t}C_{t}(j)} & = & 0\\
(1-\epsilon)\frac{Y_{t}(j)}{P_{t}C_{t}(j)} & = & \left(-\epsilon\frac{Y_{t}(j)}{P_{t}(j)}\right)\frac{1}{A_{t}}\left(N_{t}(j)\right)^{\varphi}\\
\frac{P_{t}(j)}{P_{t}} & = & \frac{\epsilon}{\epsilon-1}\frac{1}{A_{t}}C_{t}(j)(N_{t}(j))^{\varphi}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Problem
The equilibrium is defined in the standard way.
 Show that, since we do not assume the price stickiness, the following condition
s hold in equilibrium:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
P_{t}(j) & = & P_{t}\ \mbox{for all}\ j\in[0,1]\\
C_{t}(j) & = & C_{t}\ \mbox{for all}\ j\in[0,1]\\
N_{t}(j) & = & \nicefrac{C_{t}}{A_{t}}\ \mbox{for all}\ j\in[0,1]
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
The aggregate price index is defined as
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
P_{t}=\left[{\displaystyle \int_{0}^{1}}P_{t}(i)^{1-\epsilon}di\right]^{\frac{1}{1-\epsilon}}
\]

\end_inset

In equilibrium, all the firms are symmetric, so 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ i\in[0,1]:P_{t}(i)=\bar{P}_{t}$
\end_inset

.
 Hence, the aggregate price index is now
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
P_{t} & = & \left[{\displaystyle \int_{0}^{1}}\bar{P}_{t}^{1-\epsilon}di\right]^{\frac{1}{1-\epsilon}}\\
P_{t} & = & \left[\bar{P}_{t}^{1-\epsilon}{\displaystyle \int_{0}^{1}}di\right]^{\frac{1}{1-\epsilon}}\\
P_{t} & = & P_{t}(i)
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

So, the amount of individual good demanded by a yeoman farmer 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

 is
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
C_{t}(i,j) & = & \left(\frac{P_{t}(i)}{P_{t}}\right)^{-\epsilon}C_{t}(j)\\
C_{t}(i,j) & = & C_{t}(j)\ \mbox{for all}\ i\in[0,1]
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

while the consumption index is
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
C_{t}(j) & = & \left[{\displaystyle \int_{0}^{1}}C_{t}(i,j)^{\frac{\epsilon-1}{\epsilon}}di\right]^{\frac{\epsilon}{\epsilon-1}}\\
C_{t}(j) & = & \left[{\displaystyle \int_{0}^{1}}C_{t}(j)^{\frac{\epsilon-1}{\epsilon}}di\right]^{\frac{\epsilon}{\epsilon-1}}\\
C_{t}(j) & = & \left[C_{t}(j)^{\frac{\epsilon-1}{\epsilon}}{\displaystyle \int_{0}^{1}}di\right]^{\frac{\epsilon}{\epsilon-1}}\\
C_{t}(j) & = & C_{t}(j)
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

This means that aggregate demand becomes
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
C_{t} & = & {\displaystyle \int_{0}^{1}}C_{t}(i)di\\
C_{t} & = & C_{t}(j)\int_{0}^{1}di\\
C_{t} & = & C_{t}(j)\ \mbox{for all}\ j\in[0,1]
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

With 
\begin_inset Formula $P_{t}(j)=P_{t}$
\end_inset

, the demand constraint can be rewritten as
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
Y_{t}(j) & = & \left(\frac{P_{t}(j)}{P_{t}}\right)^{-\epsilon}C_{t}\\
Y_{t}(j) & = & C_{t}\ \mbox{for all}\ j\in[0,1]
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Finally, the amount of labor used for production by yeoman farmer 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

 is
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
Y_{t}(j) & = & A_{t}N_{t}(j)\\
N_{t}(j) & = & \frac{C_{t}}{A_{t}}\ \mbox{for all}\ j\in[0,1]
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Problem
Find 
\begin_inset Formula $C_{t}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{t}$
\end_inset

 in the equilibrium.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
From the results in problem 7, we can find 
\begin_inset Formula $C_{t}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{t}$
\end_inset

 in equilibrium.
 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{t}$
\end_inset

 is the aggregate demand for labor defined as
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
N_{t} & = & {\displaystyle \int_{0}^{1}}N_{t}(j)dj\\
 & = & \frac{C_{t}}{A_{t}}{\displaystyle \int_{0}^{1}}dj\\
N_{t} & = & \frac{C_{t}}{A_{t}}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Recalling the first-order necessary condition for optimality from problem
 6
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\frac{P_{t}(j)}{P_{t}} & = & \frac{\epsilon}{\epsilon-1}\frac{1}{A_{t}}C_{t}(j)(N_{t}(j))^{\varphi}\\
1 & = & \frac{\epsilon}{\epsilon-1}\frac{1}{A_{t}}C_{t}\left(\frac{C_{t}}{A_{t}}\right)^{\varphi}\\
\frac{1}{M}A^{1+\varphi} & = & C_{t}^{1+\varphi}\\
C_{t} & = & \left(\frac{1}{M}\right)^{1+\varphi}A_{t}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $M=\frac{\epsilon}{\epsilon-1}$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, the equilibrium 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{t}$
\end_inset

 is
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
N_{t} & = & C_{t}A_{t}^{-1}\\
N_{t} & = & \left(\frac{1}{M}\right)^{1+\varphi}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Problem
How is the equilibrium 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{t}$
\end_inset

 related with the technology level 
\begin_inset Formula $A_{t}$
\end_inset

? Give an economic explanation (not a math proof) for why they are related
 in such a way.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
Will prove.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Problem
Can the monetary authority affect the equilibrium 
\begin_inset Formula $C_{t}$
\end_inset

 and/or 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{t}$
\end_inset

?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
Will prove.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Problem
Is the allocation in the economy efficient? If your answer is yes, give
 a very clear explanation why you reach such a conclusion.
 If your answer is no, argue what kind of policy (that doe not have to be
 monetary policy, but could be fiscal policy or even a decree issued by
 the government) can improve welfare.
\end_layout

